Before applying an incremental change to the accessibility tree,
Ladybird’s UI process checks the whole change against the tree
it holds, and applies all of it or none of it; on a change that
doesn’t fit, it asks WebContent for the whole tree. Chromium does
the same: AXTree::Unserialize() checks every pending change in
ComputePendingChanges() before it touches the tree, and when that fails,
BrowserAccessibilityManager::OnAccessibilityEvents() hands the error
to its delegate, which recovers by creating the manager again from a
full tree. Gecko instead treats a change that doesn’t fit as a protocol
error: DocAccessibleParent::RecvShowEvent() returns IPC_FAIL for an
unknown parent or an invalid index, which kills the content process.
WebKit’s AXIsolatedTree is filled in within the web process, from the
accessibility objects it mirrors, so it has no such check to make.
